A||||9||GWMORGAN||The Easiest Way...||||9||
Z||000000||GWMORGAN||12-03-2000||09:34 AM||||I'm in the process of installing headers (F.O.C.O.A) on my '86 GT. Two bolts broke in the head (trunk side).<BR>         What's the easiest way of having them removed without taking the head off?<BR>                       TIA.<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||lowfunds||12-03-2000||12:20 PM||||Use easy outs if you have them if not you can get them at your local auto parts store||||reg||1
Z||000002||wilburt||12-03-2000||12:22 PM||||I'd drop the cradel. Two reasons: if you remove the head= head set cost, buncha labor<BR>Dropping cradel= free, less labor, easier to get at the broken bolts. <P>If you have to drill a pilot hole, it's best to be centered, and stright. Real hard to do with head in car.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||GWMORGAN||12-03-2000||01:29 PM||||Thanx for the info guys. I'll look into that "easy out" thingy. <BR>The cradle is swung down by removing the two rear bolts. I might have to completely drop the cradle in order to have it done properly. Any other suggetion(s)?||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||12-03-2000||01:31 PM|||| See if you can get/rent a right angle drill for better access.||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||12-03-2000||06:04 PM||||Use a lot of Liquid Wrench or equivalent on the bolts (before and after you break them  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ).  I've had much better luck with the square-cross-section bolt extractors, rather than the tapered /reverse-threaded "easy outs."  JMHO  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000006||lowfunds||12-03-2000||06:06 PM||||Me personally I would just remove the head to do the job corectly. Not a real big deal just replace the gasket and torque to spec when you are done. Its hard to remove broken bolts in tight areas, because using easy outs or similar methods require drilling a hole in the center of the broken bolt. But if you don't want to take the head off, Then see if you can get an angled drill as suggested. Dropping the craddle seems like alot of work to remove broken bolts( disconecting lines and hoses) but it also is an option. Just my pov good luck||||reg||1
Z||000007||Wannabe||12-04-2000||11:32 AM||||If the not-so-easy outs don't work, you can use an oxy-acetylene torch to burn the bolts out. The steel bolt will burn out long before the cast iron is affected. Then chase the threads and you're set to go. The hazards of this method are obvious. Whenever you suspect a bolt won't come out without breaking, it's a good idea to torch the ***t out of it before even trying to loosen it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||12-05-2000||01:26 PM||||Another trick is to take nut at least one size larger then the broken bolt. Hold the nut over the broken stud using locking plyers and use a wire feed mig welder to fill the hole. If done correctly, the nut will be welded to the stud and the added heat will help break up the corrosion. Then, simply use a ratchet or wrench to back the whole thing out.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000009||GWMORGAN||12-07-2000||11:59 PM||||Thanks guys for all your input. I swung the engine down, drilled out the broken bolts (4), retapped, then had the headers bolted up. The only problem is, the ocelot exhaust system won't bolt up directly where it connets to the "Y" pipe (2 bolts with the springs a little below the O2 sensor). The pipe leading into the CAT is too big.<BR>I should be able to have it fixed at the local muffler shop.<BR>Thanks again guys also to Cliff for making it possible.            GAWAMOR.||||reg||14
